think The row hands the SWEEP's timing to the fleet and names a second repair without a number. A door chosen by argument is a door nobody measured.
think Added a --doors mode to tools/fixtures/r/root_finder_scan.sh: one row per repair per build-output sentinel, every figure derived from git ls-files rather than typed.
think Reading a candidate third door -- git -C dirname($0) rev-parse --show-toplevel -- the walk's own seating comment already names its cost in words, so the lap priced it as a number rather than adopting it.
obs The sweep costs 216 files, never 189. 187 finder files plus 46 pen layouts that create rishi/bin; 17 overlap, so 29 files sit in no finder and in lanes the finder count never named.
obs The failure those 46 carry is loud rather than silent -- a pen under mktemp has no ancestor holding the new sentinel, so a copied finder reaches / and exits 2.
obs The track door costs 2 files and the ignore rule denying it is named on every pass: .gitignore:156:/rishi/bin/.
obs Both doors are RUN in the control rather than argued. A real git clone carries tracked content and nothing else, the elder finder refuses there, and with one tracked file under rishi/bin the UNCHANGED elder finder resolves.
obs My first two mutation plants were dead. Inverting the git-door filter read the truth's own number because the pen held one repo-ful and one repo-less layout; the below-root plant could not move a reading already at 0. Both repaired, and the five mutations now run on every pass rather than being recorded once in a say line.
obs I edited while the cold endurance run was in flight, so its tree_moved=yes is mine.
obs REDS FIRST, ON THE SEND'S OWN REBASE. Upstream moved 32 commits during the hot run; two finders born in them took the reading 189 to 191, so a guard green at staging is red on the same bytes after the rebase. A ratchet that only falls, over a population every ship adds to, is a wall with a delay -- the no-slack reasoning holds for a fixed population and inverts for a growing one.
obs Neither new file is sweepable from here: tools/fixtures/b/backtick_path_scan.sh stands in grass's live claim, so the one repair a lane could reach is the one the claim board forbids.
obs THE RED COULD NOT BE BOOKED. construction/REDS.md reads 65,535 against the 65,536 it declares -- one byte -- with all 23 rows OPEN and none foldable, and a row costs about 1,800. So no ship can book a red right now, on a fleet whose first law is reds first. The row is written and cited by stamp; a second bound raise is Keaton's word, so it goes to the card as an open door rather than into the file on my own authority.
